Hint : $\frac{q^2}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0 r T}=\frac{1}{\varepsilon_0} \frac{q^m a^m}{c^n}$ {taking $\frac{1}{\varepsilon_0}$ in multiplication as constant of proportionality}
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{q^2}{r T}=\frac{q^m a^m}{c^n} \\
& m=2 \\
& \frac{a^m}{c^n}=\frac{1}{C T^2}=\frac{1 a^2}{c c^2} \quad[c=a T] \\
& \frac{a^m}{c^n}=\frac{a^2}{c^3} \\
& n=3
\end{aligned}
$$
